{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Structured outputs now available in Amazon Bedrock - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a foundation model service from AWS, has introduced the capability of structured outputs. This feature ensures consistent, machine-readable responses from models that conform to defined JSON schemas. By specifying the desired format, users can receive responses that match it, making production workflows more reliable. Structured outputs are beneficial for tasks such as extracting key fields and powering workflows using APIs or tools. It reduces the need for custom validation logic and operational overhead by ensuring schema compliance, leading to fewer failed requests and retries. Structured outputs are available for Anthropic Claude 4.5 models and select open-weight models across the Converse, ConverseStream, InvokeModel, and InvokeModelWithResponseStream APIs in all commercial AWS Regions where Amazon Bedrock is supported. For more information on structured outputs and the supported models, visit the Amazon Bedrock documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/structured-outputs-available-amazon-bedrock/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Structured outputs for Claude models available on Amazon Bedrock - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock now supports structured outputs for all Anthropic Claude models, enabling schema-compliant JSON responses across both the bedrock-runtime and bedrock-mantle endpoint. This feature allows for automation of reliable tasks in various industries, such as extracting data from financial reports and compliance documents, parsing clinical notes into Electronic Health Records, enriching product catalogs, analyzing contracts, and powering intelligent support ticket routing. The structured outputs are available where Claude models are available, and users can refer to the regional availability details page for more information","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/claude-structured-outputs-bedrock/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon Bedrock now supports structured outputs to AWS GovCloud (US) Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a managed service offering access to high-performing foundation models from leading AI companies via a single API, has expanded its support for structured outputs to AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. Structured outputs enable foundation models to return consistent, schema-compliant, machine-readable responses, making it suitable for government and regulated workloads. This feature simplifies production tasks, such as extracting key fields and powering workflows, by ensuring schema compliance and minimizing failed requests and retries. Structured outputs are now generally available in all commercial AWS and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions where Amazon Bedrock is supported. To learn more about structured outputs and the supported models, visit the Amazon Bedrock documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/04/amazon-bedrock-structured-outputs-govcloud/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up or migrating Knowledge Bases to Managed, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"writing-agentcore-policy","skill_description":"Authors, validates and submits Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Policy statements (Cedar and Dogwood) for an AgentCore Gateway. Discovers the Gateway's targets and tools, generates the Gateway's Cedar schema, drafts permit/forbid, guardrail and temporal policies from access requirements or from a whole natural-language policy document (splitting it into rules, marking the rules that cannot be expressed, and repairing failing drafts in batches), checks them locally (syntax, schema, API limits, claim and guardrail pitfalls), and creates or updates them in a policy engine after explicit developer approval, reporting the final status and service findings. Use when a developer wants to write, translate, fix, validate or submit AgentCore Gateway policies, convert a policy document or a list of rules into policies, restrict which users or roles can call Gateway tools, add Bedrock guardrail or session-history (temporal) conditions, or resolve CreatePolicy/UpdatePolicy failures.\n\nServices: bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: design, deploy, debug, secure\nPersona: developer, security\nWorkload: agents, generative-ai","skill_name":"writing-agentcore-policy"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Check Bedrock structured output and model lifecycle docs"}
